//? Title: Multilayer perceptrons: nonlinear classification
//? Subtitle: Explicit tensor SGD on XOR, two moons and two petal measurements
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Learn nonlinear classification boundaries with dense tensor layers and a visible optimizer loop. The original noisy XOR, two-moons, architecture sweep and two-predictor Iris studies are retained, with train-only standardization and freshly computed metrics. Actual held-out predictions supply an annotated confusion matrix, while small tensor examples explain why nonlinear hidden features change the function class.
)

\toc

> Start with four hand-checkable XOR corners

For binary x and y, s=x+y takes values0,1,1,2. ReLU(s)−2ReLU(s−1) therefore returns0,1,1,0. This explicit tensor calculation illustrates a nonlinear feature construction without fitting. A single affine two-class decision rule cannot assign the two opposite pairs of exact corners different classes: their required half-plane inequalities contradict after summing opposite corners. This argument concerns the exact corners; it does not claim an exact50% ceiling on every finite noisy sample.

A dense hidden layer computes h=activation(XW+b); the output layer computes logits. Softmax turns logits into class probabilities, and sparse cross-entropy is the mean negative log probability assigned to the observed class. Backpropagation differentiates that scalar objective through all dense layers. The figure traces an actual2→8→2 tensor forward pass, with named layer boundaries and learnable leaves. ParameterStore initializes weights lazily; the warm-up pass creates every parameter before constructing an optimizer.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/neural_architecture.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/nn.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/tabular_classification.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto x     = n::tensor({0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1}, {4, 2});
  auto s     = n::sum(x, {1});
  auto exact = n::relu(s) - 2 * n::relu(s - 1);
  std::print("Exact tensor XOR values: {}.\n", exact.to_vector<int>());
  n::ParameterStore parameters(7);
  auto              logits = [&](const n::Tensor& input) {
    auto h = n::dense(parameters, "hidden", input, 8, n::Activation::Tanh);
    h      = n::model_stage("8 tanh features", input, h);
    auto z = n::dense(parameters, "output", h, 2);
    return n::model_stage("2 class logits", h, z);
  };
  auto graph = n::plot_model(
      logits,
      n::zeros({1, 2}),
      {.title = "Executed tensor MLP: 2 inputs, 8 tanh features, 2 logits"});
  std::print(
      "Actual learnable scalar parameters: {}; traced count {}.\n",
      parameters.count(),
      graph.parameter_count());
  graph.save_svg("build/neural_architecture.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact tensor XOR values: [0, 1, 1, 0].
Actual learnable scalar parameters: 42; traced count 42.
\image(figures/neural_architecture.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Executed tensor architecture for a two-input, eight-hidden-unit classifier.)
\alttext(The exact four-corner XOR values are computed above; the figure traces actual initialized layers rather than a fitted classifier.)

> Forward equations and the backpropagated gradient

Use row-batched notation: H₀=X has B rows and d₀ predictors. Layer l has W_l of shape d_(l−1)×d_l and bias b_l of length d_l. Hidden layers compute Z_l=H_(l−1)W_l+1b_lᵀ and H_l=φ(Z_l). Output logits omit the hidden activation. ReLU(z)=max(0,z), tanh(z) and sigmoid(z)=1/(1+exp(−z)) supply different nonlinear features. Without an intervening nonlinearity, composing affine layers collapses to one affine map, regardless of their number.

For output logits z, softmax probability is p_k=exp(z_k−m)/Σ_j exp(z_j−m), where m=max_j z_j cancels algebraically while preventing positive exponent overflow. The mean objective is −Σ_i log p_(i,y_i)/B, evaluated by native stable sparse cross-entropy. Uniform predictions have loss log K; that is a reference value, not a universal lower bound. The sampled noisy XOR classes need not have exactly identical empirical centroids, so their optimal affine loss need not equal log2 exactly.

Differentiating log Σ_j exp(z_j)−z_y gives the output error Δ_L=(P−Y)/B, with one-hot Y. Applying the chain rule backward gives Δ_l=(Δ_(l+1)W_(l+1)ᵀ)⊙φ′(Z_l). Each layer then has ∇W_l=H_(l−1)ᵀΔ_l and ∇b_l=Σ_rowsΔ_l. The factor1/B is already in Δ_L and is not applied again. Native autograd implements this reuse of forward intermediates; report code does not implement a second backpropagation routine. ReLU uses derivative zero at its kink, while tanh′=1−tanh² and sigmoid′=sigmoid·(1−sigmoid).

The visible SGD call applies θ←θ−η∇θ to all learnable leaves. An optional kernel penalty λΣ_l||W_l||²/2 would add λW_l to each kernel gradient while leaving biases unpenalized; every study here sets λ=0. Full-batch XOR makes one update per epoch, while moons and Iris shuffle rows each epoch and retain the final incomplete batch. Epoch-end loss uses the complete training subset, so it differs from simply averaging unequal-sized batch losses. Finite-budget SGD does not certify a global optimum.

A sufficiently wide hidden layer with a suitable activation can approximate continuous functions on compact domains. This existence statement does not promise that a chosen width, finite sample or optimizer finds that approximation. [Cybenko (1989), Approximation by superpositions of a sigmoidal function|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02551274] and [Hornik (1991), Approximation capabilities of multilayer feedforward networks|https://doi.org/10.1016/0893-6080(91)90009-T] provide the original report's approximation context with those qualifications.

> Retain the original600-point noisy XOR study

The native fixture retains150 points at each corner, MT19937_64 seed1, independent normal noise with SD0.12 and the original x-then-y draw order. We compare no hidden layer with8 tanh units, full-batch SGD at0.1 for800 updates, seed7 and no L2 penalty. This is a capacity experiment: every observation is a training observation. The full-data standardization is consequently fitted only to that training set. Neither accuracy nor a region plot here is an estimate of held-out generalization.

The visible loop minimizes tensor sparse softmax cross-entropy. The native tensor initialization and optimizer replace the old constructor-based stats model, so old frozen losses and percentages are recomputed rather than asserted. Both classifiers receive identical standardized inputs and training budgets. The two decision panels use raw x/y coordinates; the callback applies the frozen training statistics before tensor inference.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/neural_xor.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/nn.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/tabular_classification.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n     = dm::nn;
  namespace study = n::study;
  n::set_num_threads(2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto data    = study::xor_classification_data();
  auto scaling = study::fit_tabular_standardization(data.features);
  auto x       = study::transform_tabular_features(data.features, scaling);
  auto layout  = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  std::print(
      "| hidden units | scalar parameters | actual updates | training accuracy | final training "
      "NLL |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int width : {0, 8}) {
    n::ParameterStore parameters(7);
    auto              logits = [&](const n::Tensor& input) {
      auto h = width ? n::dense(parameters, "hidden", input, width, n::Activation::Tanh) : input;
      return n::dense(parameters, "output", h, 2);
    };
    {
      n::NoGradGuard guard;
      logits(n::take_rows(x, {0}));
    }
    n::OptimizerConfig config;
    config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Sgd;
    n::Optimizer optimizer(parameters, .1, config);
    for (int step = 0; step < 800; ++step) {
      auto objective = n::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(logits(x), data.labels);
      optimizer.minimize(objective);
    }
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    auto           z = logits(x);
    std::print(
        "| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
        width,
        parameters.count(),
        optimizer.iterations(),
        n::accuracy(z, data.labels),
        n::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(z, data.labels).item());
    auto plot = study::tabular_decision_regions(
        data.features, data.labels, data.classes, [&](const auto& raw) {
          return logits(study::transform_tabular_features(raw, scaling));
        });
    plot.title(width ? "Noisy XOR: 8 tanh units" : "Noisy XOR: affine logits")
        .x_label("x")
        .y_label("y");
    layout.add(plot);
  }
  layout.fit_panels().save_svg("build/neural_xor.svg");
}
```
